From 01403fe918b7aaa11162576befd1a1e535499e1e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cohost u3 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 16:13:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 15a-16: in-place walks in every runtime walker; no lock on a
 realized lazy seq or range

Draft: doc/upstream/jank-15-reduce-walk.md (part one) and jank-16-lazy-mutex.md in
johnmn3/cohost.cljc. Repros: bash dev/research/upstream/run.sh 15-reduce-walk,
run.sh 16-lazy-mutex (Expected: reduce + over a vector near reduce-kv; first on a
realized lazy seq near first on a list).

- sequence_range: the walk takes fresh_seq() once and steps with next_in_place()
  while it owns the seq (object.hpp's ownership rule); a step that hands off to
  another object (a shared tail, a cached chunk) takes a fresh_seq() of it if it
  steps in place, else steps it with next(); lazy seqs are unwrapped, so no lazy
  wrapper is stepped; chunked_cons is stepped with next() (its next_in_place does
  not cross a chunk boundary). Every make_sequence_range walker gets it: reduce,
  str, sort, vec, sequence_length, sequence_equal, equal, nth, hashing, apply_to,
  print/println/pr/prn, the collection constructors.
- lazy_sequence: an atomic realized flag (release on realize, acquire on read);
  seq/first/next/fresh_seq take no lock once realized. next_in_place for a lazy
  seq made by fresh_seq (owned); any other lazy seq steps with next(). The
  sequence_like_in_place behavior bit is set.
- range: chunk_ready/next_ready atomic flags; next(), chunked_first(),
  chunked_next() lock only to build the chunk or the cached next; next_in_place
  (owner only) takes no lock.

Files: include/cpp/jank/runtime/sequence_range.hpp, src/cpp/jank/runtime/sequence_range.cpp,
include/cpp/jank/runtime/obj/lazy_sequence.hpp, src/cpp/jank/runtime/obj/lazy_sequence.cpp,
include/cpp/jank/runtime/obj/range.hpp, src/cpp/jank/runtime/obj/range.cpp.
